 ## Background
-MoCo-NeRF was proposed by J. Kim et al. in 2024 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of conventional spatial offset modeling in dynamic human rendering, which suffers from high learning complexity and misaligned objectives [S1]. The method builds on the principles of canonical transformation and conditioning described in [Parent](18108-a-nerf.md), specifically utilizing inverse linear blend skinning for rigid transformations [S1]. It is evaluated on the ZJU-MoCap and MonoCap datasets [S1].
+MoCo-NeRF was proposed by J. Kim et al. in 2024 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of conventional spatial offset modeling in dynamic human rendering, which suffers from high learning complexity and misaligned objectives [S1]. The method builds on the principles of canonical transformation and conditioning described in [Dynamic and Articulated Scene Modeling](18119-dynamic-and-articulated-scene-modeling.md), specifically utilizing inverse linear blend skinning for rigid transformations [S1]. It is evaluated on the ZJU-MoCap and MonoCap datasets [S1].
 
